{
  "key": "horsegenetics.coral_bloom",
  "name": "Coral Bloom",
  "phase": "magical",
  "priority": 279,
  "rarity": "RARE",
  "blurb": "The coat ruffles into overlapping coral lobes, each rimmed in bright orange and shading from deep sea-blue to a pale crown, flecked all over with gold.",
  "notes": [
    "The three zones of a lobe are three readings of ONE tessellation - the same seed, scale and warp, with only the 'gap' moved - so the rim, the fill and the crown share a wall rather than each finding their own and opening seams between them. A band is the wider reading multiplied in and the narrower one subtracted out; the rim is the narrowest reading inverted, which is the crack itself.",
    "The warp is what turns CRACKLE's naturally straight facets into rounded, ruffled lobes. Under close inspection the tessellation is still a Voronoi cell with flat sides; at coat scale it reads as coral, which is the whole of what it was tuned for.",
    "No hue knob. The orange rim over blue fill IS the coral idea rather than a setting on it, and a horse wanting a different palette wants a different gene."
  ],
